u_{\eta,y}$ is stationary for this process. In particular, the returned position $x_{\pi}$ is well-defined almost surely. The well-posedness and stationarity assertions are proved in Section A.1 . The induced Markov kernel on positions also satisfies the following reversibility property. Proposition 3.2 (Reversibility of the conditional half-turn kernel) . For every $y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ , let $\mathcal{H}_{y}$ be the Markov kernel on $\mathbb{R}^{d}$ induced by Algorithm 1 ; thus $\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\cdot)$ is the law of the returned position $x_{\pi}$ for input $(x,y)$ . Then $\mathcal{H}_{y}$ is reversible with respect to $\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}$ . The proof is deferred to Appendix A.2 . The following proposition quantifies the stationary bounce count of the ideal half-turn process. Proposition 3.3 (Stationary bounce count) . There exists a universal constant $K_{m id}\geq 1$ such that the following holds. Suppose that $(x_{0},Y)\sim\pi_{\eta}$ . Conditional on $Y$ , let $\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y}$ be independent of $x_{0}$ , and let $p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)$ be independent of all position variables. If $N_{m b}^{m id}$ denotes the number of bounce events in the resulting ideal half-turn process of Algorithm 1 , then <
